#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""upwatch: check things you care about for new versions (GitHub releases and commits, Nexus Mods pages, and
lists of mods kept in other files), print what changed and optionally pop up a desktop notification.

Config: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/upwatch/config.toml (default ~/.config/upwatch/config.toml); `upwatch init` writes an
example. State (what you have acknowledged, what was notified): $XDG_STATE_HOME/upwatch/state.json. Caches:
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/upwatch/. Only the Python standard library is used (Python 3.11 or newer for tomllib).

usage:
  upwatch [check] [NAME ...] [--top] [--tag TAG ...] [--games] [-v] [--json] [--notify] [--quiet] [--wait-network SECS]
  upwatch [check] [NAME ...] [...] --fetch [FETCH OPTIONS]   check, then fetch the Nexus file updates (see below)
  upwatch fetch WANTS.json [FETCH OPTIONS]                    fetch the files a want-list names
  upwatch list [NAME ...] [--top] [--tag TAG ...] [--games]      show what would be checked (no network)
  upwatch ack NAME ...       accept the latest version of NAME as yours (for sources without an installed version)
  upwatch init               write an example config if none exists

NAME is a source name from the config ("ge-proton", "tw3"), a list member as SOURCE/ID ("tw3/modSmartCamera"), or a
member's id or label alone. --top checks the standalone sources only and skips what lists point to; --tag keeps
sources carrying that tag (a list's members inherit its tags); --games is --tag game. --wait-network SECS waits up to
SECS seconds for the network (a TCP connection to the hosts the sources use, tried every 30 s) before checking, and skips
the check quietly if it never comes up: the timer uses it right after login, while the network is still coming up.
With --notify, lookups that fail because the network is down are never notified; other failures (refused key, gone
repo) are, once until they work again.
--fetch (in a terminal only; never with --notify, --quiet or --json) runs the check, then opens the Nexus file page of
each update (not held, not failed) in your browser, a batch at a time, waits for YOUR click on each download, and moves
each finished file it recognises from your Downloads folder to the fetch_dir of its source (or list). upwatch never
downloads anything itself. A source without fetch_dir (and no fetch_dir in [settings]) is not fetched: one line says so.
FETCH OPTIONS: --dry-run (show, open and move nothing), --yes (move matching files already in Downloads without asking),
--batch N (pages per batch, default 10, at most 20), --gap MIN MAX (random seconds between two page openings, default
1 2, MIN at least 1), --downloads DIR, --settle SECS (a file must hold still this long, default 2), --poll SECS,
--report FILE (the full result as JSON), --opener CMD (open pages with CMD URL); for `fetch FILE` also --inbox-dir DIR
and --archive-dir DIR. With -v the fetch summary also gives each file's path, how it was recognised and its md5.
Fetch exit status: 0 everything in place, 1 something missing or not moved, 2 bad options or list, 130 Ctrl-C.
--json prints one object per item: path, name (the name shown in the text output), and the item's own fields (a
Nexus list member's mod page title is nexus_name; latest_size is the latest file's size in bytes, when Nexus gives it).

Output, one line per item:  "U name (installed -> latest)  link"  update;  ". name (version)"  up to date (-v only);
"= name (...)"  newer version held back on purpose (-v only);  "! name: ..."  archive not found on Nexus for this mod;
"E name: ..."  the lookup failed.
"""
